# Routing only. No credential: neither registry asks for one, and an install needs none — only
# `npm publish` has a client-side pre-flight, and nothing here publishes.
#
# TWO SERVICES since the byte-plane split. mirror.dev.localhost:8080 is qits-platform-mirror, which
# holds the npmjs pull-through cache; registry.dev.localhost:8080 is qits-artifacts, which hosts
# the @qits scope. Both addresses are edge vhosts on the local platform, and they exist for the one
# consumer this file has: a developer on the deployment host. No build reads it — every build runs
# in a container on qits-net, where these addresses do not exist, and takes the qits-net aliases
# from the environment.
#
# CI overrides this file rather than editing it: the pipeline sets npm_config_registry /
# npm_config_@qits:registry from $QITS_NPM_PROXY_URL / $QITS_NPM_REGISTRY_URL, and environment
# outranks both a project and a user .npmrc.
#
# The lockfile is a separate problem, and it is npm's: npm records an absolute `resolved` URL per
# package, so package-lock.json spells these addresses hundreds of times.
# .config/qits/ci-event-release-request.yml retargets those origins before `npm ci`, and the
# comment there explains why npm's own `replace-registry-host` cannot do it.
registry=http://mirror.dev.localhost:8080/artifacts/npm/npmjs/
@qits:registry=http://registry.dev.localhost:8080/artifacts/npm/npm/
